  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output A.1. More accurate and sustainable statistics on decent work using the latest statistical standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Liberian Government has finalised the Labour Force Survey report in November 2021. The second labour force survey (LFS) was conducted in Liberia in 2017, but the data was not analysed and finalised. In order to finalise the report, from October to December 2020, the Liberia Institute of Statistics and Geo-Information System (LISGIS) cleaned and edited the survey data. Then, from February to August 2021, LISGIS and Ministry of Labour (MoL) computed the survey weights.  The findings of this survey provided an overview of the state of labour market before the outbreak of Covid-19. In addition, the survey findings updated the decent work indicators for Liberia using the latest statistical standards. To support the efforts of the government to finalize the survey activities, the ILO provided with LISGIS and MoL a detailed list of internal inconsistencies to be cleaned and edited in the survey data. ILO produced the survey weights in August and shared them with LISGIS and MoL. The ILO also developed a methodology to produce the survey weights. This methodology was used to compute the survey weights and derive preliminary findings from the labour force survey. In this process, capacities of LISGIS and MoL were developed in data processing with a focus on cleaning, editing and weighting the LFS data. Two technical meetings were organised with national partners and UNDP with the support of the ILO to present and discuss a detailed list of internal inconsistencies to be cleaned and edited in the survey data. A list of variables to be used to build key labour market indicators were defined in consultation with LISGIS and Ministry of Labour.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">A.1.1. Number of member States that strengthen their labour market statistics, standards and information systems through improved statistical surveys and use of other statistical sources</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
